{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And you see among the captives a woman of beautiful form.</b> Now from the verse's saying <b>beautiful form</b> it implies specifically one of beautiful form, yet from the verse's saying <b>and you desire her</b> they expounded in Sifrei: even an ugly one. And this is surprising — why should it say ״beautiful form״ and then need to include an ugly one? It should have said ״and you see a woman״ and written neither ״beautiful form״ nor ״and you desire.״" "<b>And to settle</b> the verse, it is necessary to awaken [a question] regarding this matter: why should HaShem command such things, to defile a person with a foreign daughter, and especially at the time of the miracle's performance, when one ought to add purity and attachment to HaShem — [yet] He permits doing an act of ugliness hateful before Him, blessed be He, and this will bring about the distancing of our attachment to Him, blessed be He. With the prohibition against eating forbidden food, by contrast, we find that the Rambam, of blessed memory (Hilchot Melachim, chapter 8), gave a reason: if he is hungry — and that is a sound reason. But this act will make its doer abhorrent. And our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Kiddushin 21b): ״The Torah spoke only against the evil inclination״ — and it would be fitting to subdue it and eradicate it from the heart of the holy people at that time, when their King is at their head to wage war for them." "<b>However,</b> the foundation of the matter and its sod is according to their words, of blessed memory (Zohar Chadash, Balak 53), that they said: through the sin of the first Adam, many precious souls were taken captive in the hands of the sitra achra, and these are the souls of converts. Go out and learn how very many great ones of the world come from the nations, and Ruth the Moabitess testifies, and how many great ones of the world — Shemayah and Avtalyon and Onkelos the convert, and many more like these." "<b>Further,</b> I will reveal to you one sod: you will find a pure soul attached to an impure soul, and the pure one has no power to bend the impure one toward good, and it lies there until the time of freedom. Go out and learn something analogous from the soul of Rabbi Chanina ben Teradyon, which was attached in Shechem ben Chamor, as hinted in the word ״רַחֲבַת יָדַיִם״ (Genesis 34:21), as stated in the words of the mekubalim (Likutei Torah, Vayishlach). And we find that that soul accomplished no good deed in Shechem, and it departed; and when he cleaved to Dinah, the soul found from her and cleaved to him, as it says (Genesis 34:3): ״וַתִּדְבַּק נַפְשׁוֹ בְּדִינָה.״ And keep for yourself this principle. And there is a holy soul within the kelipah, and in the place of its dwelling it bends the heart of her who dwells within it toward good, and they either expel the evil portion or sweeten it. And these are the converts who convert from the nations of their own accord, such as Ruth the Moabitess and Naamah, and as we have seen with our own eyes — converts who come of themselves and convert." "<b>Now,</b> I have already written many times that through the performance of a mitzvah, the light of the Shechinah clothes itself in a person and expels from him the evil portion, as it says (Ecclesiastes 8:5): ״שׁוֹמֵר מִצְוָה לֹא יֵדַע דָּבָר רָע״ — and especially emissaries of a mitzvah. And see what I explained in parashat Shelach, that emissaries of a mitzvah at the time of their mission — even the evil inclination, their mitzvah saves them. And likewise one who engages in a transgression — a spirit of impurity clothes itself in him and perverts his way." "<b>And after</b> HaShem has informed us of all this, the sayings of El Elyon will shine like the sun at noonday in this commandment — for He prefaced <b>כִּי תֵצֵא לַמִּלְחָמָה</b> etc., meaning that his going out is for the sake of a commandment, as we explained above. It further says: if he saw among the captives — meaning at the time of captivity — and as indeed Rambam was precise in chapter 8 of the Laws of Kings, that at that moment he is still engaged in the commandment. If he sees an אֵשֶׁת יְפַת תֹּאַר — meaning a married woman — and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Kiddushin 21b) אֵשֶׁת אִישׁ, and their words are true, except that if the verse intended their meaning alone it should have said אֵשֶׁת אִישׁ and not אֵשֶׁת יְפַת תֹּאַר. Rather, it comes to intimate that in the very midst of his engagement in the commandment, HaShem will open his eyes to perceive in the gentile woman that she possesses יְפַת תֹּאַר, which is the holy soul called יְפַת תֹּאַר, for the radiance of holy souls is wondrous and mighty. And this woman has acquired this good portion within herself, and therefore Scripture calls her ״אֵשֶׁת יְפַת תֹּאַר.״ And your recognition of it is through the fact that you see you are drawn to desire her at a time when you are cleaving to the Shekhinah in the very midst of your being occupied with the mission of a commandment, as mentioned — this will awaken you that it is for a good thing that you have desir ed." "<b>And be precise:</b> it says <b>בָהּ</b> and does not say אוֹתָהּ, to intimate that it is not the woman herself that you desire but rather what is within her — which is יְפַת תֹּאַר, meaning the good portion that resembles the pure soul. And accordingly, even as to the woman's outward appearance she falls within the permitted, for יְפַת תֹּאַר does not revert to the revealed husk — since toward that an Israelite's desire would not be drawn in this state, as we have said." "And it says <b>וְלָקַחְתָּ לְךָ לְאִשָׁה</b>. Now, in every statement concerning her Scripture uses a language of designation: ״וַהֲבֵאתָהּ״ etc., ״וּבְעַלְתָּהּ״ etc., ״וְשִׁלַּחְתָּהּ״ — but here it says <b>וְלָקַחְתָּ</b>. Scripture changed its language to intimate to you that the taking is of the concealed element, which is the holy soul; that in the course of the matter there will be in her another kind of taking, whereby he will merit יְפַת תֹּאַר. For when an Israelite man cleaves to a woman in whom there is a portion of the good, immediately his place will acquire that good portion within Israel and he will merit it — as Dinah daughter of Jacob merited a holy soul through Shechem son of Hamor's encounter with her." "<b>And now:</b> since there are two aspects to the attachment of holiness within the husk, as we mentioned above, and regarding this woman it is not known whether the attached portion within her is something that departs through the first act of attachment, as mentioned, and the woman remains fully absorbed in the evil portion and is emptied of the good portion — or perhaps she is entirely absorbed in the good portion, cut off and pleasant — therefore HaShem commands <b>וַהֲבֵאתָהּ אֶל תּוֹךְ בֵּיתֶךָ</b> etc., and He will distance from her the portion of attachment to the aspect of evil. And then the woman will be tested after thirty days: if this his wife converts, then she is indeed cut off and pleasant — and that is the meaning of His saying <b>וְהָיְתָה לְךָ לְאִשָׁה</b>, if he desires. And if he does not desire etc. — this is your sign that the good portion that was in her departed through the first act and she was left with the evil portion." "And since there is room for a person to desire a foreign woman in a way of harlotry — for we did not say that he would not desire a foreign woman, but only if she has a holy soul, and specifically while he is engaged in a commandment as we wrote, but not so afterward — therefore HaShem commanded <b>וְגִלְּחָה אֶת רֹאשָׁהּ וְעָשְׂתָה אֶת צִפָּרְנֶיהָ</b> etc., and all the things that repulse the woman, in order to remove natural desire. And if she converted and he desires her — this is your sign that the evil portion was sweetened and absorbed away from her, and therefore ״וְהָיָה לְךָ לְאִשָׁה.״ And if he does not desire <b>בָּהּ</b> — Scripture was precise to say <b>בָּהּ</b>, to intimate that the desire is for what is within her, which is the good portion." "<b>And you see among the captives a woman of beautiful form.</b> He was precise in saying <b>for her soul</b>, to give a reason why he must send her away and not sell her and benefit from her value, since she is part of the captives that HaShem has given him. And he says the reason is ״for her soul״ — meaning, for the sake of her soul, for she is a woman of beautiful form, as was alluded to at the outset, after he has benefited from her. Or it may be on this track: <b>for her soul</b> — meaning the impure soul of the gentile woman, to exclude [the rest], for the pure soul of the woman of beautiful form you have already won, and nothing remains in her except the gentile soul." "And his saying <b>and sell — you shall not sell her</b>: for HaShem desires that Israel not conduct themselves with the aspect of evil in this manner, on the principle of what they said (Psalms 69:27): ״אֲשֶׁר הִכִּיתָ רָדְפוּ״ — that the holy spirit is incensed against the nations because after they see Israel struck, they pursue them. And this is analogous: after he has extracted and swallowed [the good] from her, he shall no longer benefit from her money." "<b>And it concludes</b> by saying <b>because you afflicted her</b> — meaning, you have taken from her wealth and goodness, which is [what makes her] a woman of beautiful form, and you have made her poor, you have made her afflicted, you have made her turbid. And this further testifies to our point, for is it possible in the world that an Israelite who lies with a gentile woman is afflicting her? Have they not said, of blessed memory (Avodah Zarah 22b): their animals are more beloved to them [the gentiles] than their wives, and their wives more than themselves — so what affliction is this for the gentile woman? Rather, the intent is the hidden affliction, which is the despoiling of holiness from her." "<b>And by way of</b> remez (allusion), the passage may be explained on this path: Behold, the entire structure of the world and its continuance depends on the conduct of the people of Israel — if they improve their ways, the world stands, and the heavens rejoice and the earth exults, and HaShem our Elohim rejoices and exults in us. And the uprightness of Israel's path depends on victory over the evil inclination. And Scripture comes to awaken man to the knowledge that when he departs from the supernal world to enter this world, he must be prepared for battle, for he must not think that no fortification is needed for this victory — on the contrary, it is precisely this that is called מלחמה (war), on the principle of their saying (Avot 4:1): 'Who is mighty? One who conquers his inclination' — for there is no might greater than this. And therefore it says <b>to the war</b> — with a pataḥ under the lamed." "<b>And you see among the captives a woman of beautiful form</b> — it comes to awaken [us] that this war is not like a warrior who fights to capture a city, and when he grows weary of the battle he turns and goes on his way; rather, [it is] like a man fighting his enemy, who, if he is negligent, [the enemy] will rise against him and strike him. So too, if a man is negligent in fighting the enemy, [the enemy] will rise against him and destroy him from the world. And Scripture promises him that even though the inclination is powerful with human force, nevertheless HaShem delivers him into his hand. And he says <b>and you capture his captivity</b> — he was precise in saying ״his captivity״ and not ״him״, for he has no substance in himself and his absence is what is hoped for; rather, ״his captivity״ — meaning that which he has captured, which is the aspect of the good from you and from those like you, through his enticement and his victory, for how many souls of the people of Israel are held captive in his hand." "<b>And its saying וְרָאִיתָ בַּשִּׁבְיָה אֵשֶׁת יְפַת תֹּאַר</b>: it says אֵשֶׁת in the construct form, meaning the soul of a person, for the ס״מ has no dominion over the soul until it descends below and enters into a person — through him it is taken captive. The soul is called אִשָּׁה, and he calls it יְפַת תֹּאַר, for the soul is exceedingly beautiful, except that it grows darkened through its evil deeds; and when the evil inclination is vanquished, then a person will perceive in his soul how beautiful and how pleasant it is." "<b>וְרָאִיתָ בַּשִּׁבְיָה אֵשֶׁת יְפַת תֹּאַר וְחָשַׁקְתָּ בָהּ</b> — meaning: then the desire that he had through the words of the evil inclination and its enticements, he will clothe them upon it in order to repair for it the intelligibilia and the enduring world, all of which is good." "And its saying <b>וַהֲבֵאתָהּ אֶל תּוֹךְ בֵּיתֶךָ</b>: for through the enticement of evil the soul becomes distanced from the body and an evil portion dwells in its place, as it says (Job 18:4) ״טוֹרֵף נַפְשׁוֹ בְּאַפּוֹ״. And the body is called a house, in the manner of their saying (Ta'anit 11a): Who testifies concerning a person? The beams of his house — which is the body. And when he vanquishes his inclination, he brings his soul into his house and is called a master of soul. And since the soul that was acquired from an impure place needs to be cleansed from the diseases of evil that adhere to it, which is the aspect of the hair of the head, therefore it says <b>וְגִלְּחָה אֶת רֹאשָׁהּ</b>. Also the aspect of the kelippah alluded to in the nails — concerning it he said, corresponding to it, <b>וְעָשְׂתָה אֶת צִפָּרְנֶיהָ</b> — an expression of repair, to remove the veil of impurity that cloaks the soul from the spirit of impurity. And this is its saying <b>וְהֵסִירָה אֶת שִׂמְלַת שִׁבְיָהּ מֵעָלֶיהָ</b> — meaning that which she acquired from her captivity in an impure place. And this will come about through the scouring of sins and the ways of repentance and the preparation of the mind toward Elohim of Israel, blessed be He. And he chooses a dwelling place for her in a house designated for Israel, which is the beit midrash, and this is its saying <b>וְיָשְׁבָה בְּבֵיתֶךָ</b>. And she confesses with weeping over having betrayed her father and her mother and over her separation from them — her father is the Holy One, blessed be He; her mother is Knesset Yisrael (Berakhot 35a). יֶרַח יָמִים — this is the measure sufficient for weeping; or it may allude to the month designated for repentance, which is the month of Elul." "<b>וְאַחַר כֵּן תָּבוֹא אֵלֶיהָ.</b> Meaning: you will enter to enjoy from its light, in the sod of the saying ״נֶפֶשׁ עָמֵל עָמְלָה לוֹ״ (Proverbs 16:26), and it will assist him to stand on the pleasant path, and you will be called its master, for you acquired it through the power of your deeds, and this is its saying <b>וּבְעַלְתָּהּ</b>. Also for the day of revival she will be designated for you, to return to your body and to stand in it, and this is its saying <b>וְהָיְתָה לְךָ לְאִשָּׁה</b>." "And the verse returns to command concerning the soul: that if the man's heart did not incline to hope to do good for his soul and to strengthen himself to illuminate for it in the supernal world, in any event he must be careful not to harm it, in the manner that Solomon said (Ecclesiastes 12:7) ״וְהָרוּחַ תָּשׁוּב אֶל הָאֱלֹהִים אֲשֶׁר נְתָנָהּ״, and they expounded in tractate Shabbat (152b): give it back to Him as He gave it to you. And this is its saying <b>אִם לֹא חָפַצְתָּ בָּהּ וְשִׁלַּחְתָּהּ לְנַפְשָׁהּ</b> — meaning that you not deliver her into the hand of a שַׁבַּאי, but rather free, with no ruling hand upon her." "<b>Or</b> it may intend according to what they said in the Zohar (vol. 3, 67a) on the verse ״נַפְשִׁי אִוִּיתִיךָ בַּלַּיְלָה״ (Isaiah 26:9), that concerning the Shekhinah he says ״נַפְשִׁי״, and this is what he alluded to by saying <b>וְשִׁלַּחְתָּהּ לְנַפְשָׁהּ</b> — that is, the Shekhinah, which shall return to the place from which she went out. And his saying <b>וּמָכֹר לֹא תִמְכְּרֶנָּה</b> etc. is according to what they interpreted in the Zohar (vol. 2, 97a) on the verse ״לְעַם נָכְרִי לֹא יִמְשֹׁל לְמָכְרָהּ״ (Exodus 21:8). And his saying <b>בַּכָּסֶף</b> is because a person might say: how could a person exercise dominion over the soul to sell it? Therefore it says ״בַּכָּסֶף״ — denoting כּוֹסֶף (longing), for through the intensity of desire and longing in the hearts of the children of man they sell their soul into the hand of the enemy. And we alluded to this from the verse ״אֶת כַּסְפְּךָ לֹא תִתֵּן לוֹ בְּנֶשֶׁךְ״ etc. (Leviticus 25:37). And his saying <b>לֹא תִתְעַמֵּר</b> — as Onkelos translates: ״לָא תִתַּגַר״, meaning: do not provoke against her the adversaries who wage war against the sinning soul — <b>תַּחַת אֲשֶׁר עִנִּיתָהּ</b>, for sin is called, in the scale of the soul, עִנּוּי (affliction)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:19:47.918146126Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}